ATTACK 3 — Chart Living bars going negative in a net-refund month. Read
buildBudgetVsActualChartData in full: `livingValues = append(livingValues, livingMonth)` has no
clamp; a negative value flows straight into the Plotly bar trace's `y` array with no NaN/Inf
risk (finite arithmetic, no division in this path). Plotly bar charts render negative values as
downward bars natively — a legitimate visual, not a broken contract. Not a defect.

ATTACK 4 — summarize_spending's documented non-negative conventions vs a signed
PlanExcludedTotal. The MCP tool's own doc string (read directly, not paraphrased) says
"living_monthly_actual EXCLUDES the Health Insurance category" without claiming
non-negativity, and the budget2 MCP server's own top-level instructions document that
summarize_spending's by_category/by_merchant/by_month rows "can go NEGATIVE when refunds
outweigh spending in that row" — so a signed, occasionally-negative intermediate is already
within this tool's established conventions. PlanExcludedTotal itself is not yet surfaced in
any MCP output (grepped summaryOutput's fields — not present), so no direct sign-contract
violation at the API surface today. Superseded by Attack 6 below regardless (living_monthly_actual
itself is wrong in the new counterexample, independent of sign-documentation).

ATTACK 5 — Re-checked attempt-1's clean angles against the new code paths: HI-overlap
precedence (PlanExcludedOutflows' HI-continue-before-map-lookup ordering is untouched by this
attempt's diff — confirmed via hunk boundaries), twin-Hash leak (deduplicateTransactions
unchanged, re-confirmed at loader.go:717-732), Comparison() map reuse (still threaded
unmodified to both Calculate calls, now with a sign-divergent fixture proving both windows
apply it identically). All still clean.

ATTACK 6 — THE SIGNED-NET FIX IS ALGEBRAICALLY INCOMPLETE (CONFIRMED DEFECT, NEW
COUNTEREXAMPLE). Every fixed site computes `livingTotal`/`livingMonth`/`spend` as
`(componentAbsTotal) - healthcareTotal - planExcludedTotal` where `planExcludedTotal` is now
correctly SIGNED but is still subtracted from an INDEPENDENTLY-Abs'd combined total
(`totalExpenses = math.Abs(outflows.SumAmount())`, taken over ALL outflows including the
flagged and HI rows). Let S = the signed net of the "living remainder" (non-HI, non-flagged
outflows) and F = the flagged group's signed net. Algebraically,
`livingTotal = |S+F| - H + F` (H=healthcareTotal, 0 here). This equals the CORRECT answer
`|S|` if and only if S<0 AND S+F<0 -- i.e., only when the living remainder ITSELF nets to real
spend (negative), which is the ONLY sign combination the ruling's original counterexample and
all four of this attempt's new sign-divergent tests exercise (S always negative/net-spend, only
F's sign varies). When the LIVING REMAINDER itself nets to a refund across the window (S>=0) --
independent of the flagged group's own sign -- the formula is wrong.

PROOF (throwaway probe, added/run/deleted inside internal/services/metrics/, deletion confirmed
via `git status --porcelain` before writing this verdict; ran the real, fixed Calculate()):
  Fixture: $8000 income; living-remainder (non-flagged, non-HI) bucket = one -$1000 grocery
  spend plus one +$4000 Outflow-typed credit/adjustment (positive amount, description with no
  income-keyword match -- classifyTransaction's own documented default keeps such a row typed
  Outflow and positive; this construction technique -- hand-typing TransactionType/Amount
  directly -- is the SAME one every test in this run, including the worker's own, already
  uses). Net for this bucket = +3000 (a REFUND overall for the living remainder). Alongside a
  completely ORDINARY flagged group: a single -$500 car payment (net spend, NOT a refund --
  the case attempt 1's original, uncontested test already covered in isolation).
  Expected LivingExpensesTotal (the flagged $500 payment excluded entirely, whatever the
  remainder's own sign): |(-1000)+4000| = 3000.
  Actual result from the real, attempt-2-fixed Calculate():
    LivingExpensesTotal = 2000.00 (want 3000.00); PlanExcludedTotal = 500.00 (correct sign,
    +500, an ordinary net-spend flagged group)
    DEFECT: LivingExpensesTotal = 2000.00, want 3000.00
  PlanExcludedTotal's OWN sign is correct (+500, confirmed) -- this is not attempt 1's bug
  recurring. The defect is a different, more general one: the subtraction-from-an-already-abs'd
  total architecture only cancels correctly when the "other" component's sign matches the
  combined total's sign, and attempt 2 patched the specific case where the FLAGGED group's sign
  diverges, not the general case where the LIVING REMAINDER's sign diverges. None of the
  attempt-2 sign-divergent tests (grepped and read all four in
  plan_exclusions_signed_net_test.go, plus the chart/render/spend-package equivalents) vary the
  living remainder's own sign -- every fixture keeps it negative (real spend) and only varies
  the flagged group's sign, so this gap was never exercised.
  This is not a contrived/unreachable input: a large store credit, price adjustment, or
  reimbursement-shaped row (typed Outflow, positive amount, no income-keyword hit) landing in a
  slow-spending month is at least as plausible as ruling SY-2026-08-30c's own accepted
  counterexample (a flagged loan group's net refund) -- if anything more so, since the living
  bucket spans far more transaction variety than one flagged def's matches.

Fix, still confined to SY4's own touched code: replace the "compute totalExpenses via one Abs
over everything, then subtract independently-Abs'd/signed component totals" pattern with
"filter outflows down to the correctly-scoped subset FIRST (excluding HI rows and flagged-map
rows), sum that subset's signed amounts, take ONE final Abs" -- exactly mirroring how
totalExpenses itself and healthcareTotal are each already computed over their own scoped
subsets. This removes the sign-interaction entirely rather than patching one manifestation of
it, and the same restructuring applies uniformly to the four fixed sites (metrics.go's range
total/trend/walk, handlers.go's chart per-month value).

Own build/test re-verification: `go test ./internal/services/metrics/...
./internal/handlers/dashboard/... ./internal/services/mcpsvc/spend/... -count=1` all `ok` (the
shipped suite is internally consistent for the sign combination it tests; it does not exercise
the combination that breaks).

STRONGEST ATTACK THAT FAILED TO BREAK IT: Attack 2 (combined cumulative walk reconciliation)
-- the walk recomputes its own signed-net subtraction independently from LivingExpensesTotal,
which is exactly the kind of place a second, drifted implementation could disagree; it stays
internally consistent (invariant holds) for every sign combination this attempt actually
tests, including the ruling's own net-refund fixture.

VERDICT: FAIL on Attack 6 -- the signed-net fix genuinely repairs ruling SY-2026-08-30c's exact
counterexample but is algebraically incomplete: it still produces a wrong LivingExpensesTotal
(and every figure derived the same way: per-month trend, chart Living bars, cumulative walk,
summarize_spending's living_monthly_actual) whenever the LIVING REMAINDER itself nets to a
refund across the window, independent of the flagged group's own sign -- a materially different
and independently plausible scenario the new sign-divergent test suite does not cover.
